Line 65:
  BEFORE: Third, these theories make moral disagreement difficult to understand. On the individual subjectivist view, $A$ could disagree with $B$'s statement, $x$ is wrong', only if $A$ doubted that $B$ was truthfully reporting $B$'s attitudes. On the cultural relativist view, the disagreement could only occur if the parties disagreed about what the attitudes of most of society were, and it would be impossible for anyone to criticize the prevailing social norms. On the divine command theory, moral disagreements would have to be disagreements about God's commands, and atheists and agnostics thus could not have moral views.$^1$
  AFTER:  Third, these theories make moral disagreement difficult to understand. On the individual subjectivist view, $A$ could disagree with $B$'s statement, '$x$ is wrong', only if $A$ doubted that $B$ was truthfully reporting $B$'s attitudes. On the cultural relativist view, the disagreement could only occur if the parties disagreed about what the attitudes of most of society were, and it would be impossible for anyone to criticize the prevailing social norms. On the divine command theory, moral disagreements would have to be disagreements about God's commands, and atheists and agnostics thus could not have moral views.$^1$
