Line 67:
  BEFORE: Fourth, these theories all imply that a merely arbitrary attitude or decision can render an action obligatory. All of them imply that there are no evaluative facts independent of the attitudes of the specified individual or group. Therefore, the specified individual or group can not approve of the things they approve of because they are right, good, or have any other positive characteristic. Thus, these attitudes of approval would have to be arbitrary. But how would someone's arbitrarily approving of $x$ make it right to do $x$?
  AFTER:  Fourth, these theories all imply that a merely arbitrary attitude or decision can render an action obligatory. All of them imply that there are no evaluative facts independent of the attitudes of the specified individual or group. Therefore, the specified individual or group can not approve of the things they approve of because they are right, good, or have any other positive characteristic. Thus, these attitudes of approval would have to be arbitrary. But how would someone's arbitrarily approving of $x$ make it right to do $x$?
